# Battle City remastered - level 2
#
# One character is one cell, one line is one row. The grid decides the map size:
# every row must be the same length, and this one is 58 x 50 (cols x rows).
# Four cells across and four down make one classic brick block - that is what lets
# a wall crumble by quarters instead of vanishing whole.
#
# 0  empty        4  fortress (the wall around the eagle)
# 1  brick        5  water
# 2  steel        6  bush
# 3  eagle        7  ice
#
# Letters lay a bonus on the map. It lies there until someone takes it or shoots it - unlike the ones
# that drop during a match, it has no lifetime. The cell under it stays free ground, and the bonus
# itself is the size of a tank, so it covers the cells right and below the one that marks it.
#
# t  timer        l  extra tank     c  caliber
# h  helmet       s  star           b  ship
# g  grenade      v  shovel
# A capital letter lays the same bonus golden - B is a golden ship.
#
# One exception to "one character is one cell": 3 is the eagle's top-left corner and covers 3x3
# cells on its own, so the 8 cells right and below it must be left as 0 - they are its body, not
# free space. Everything else, the fortress ring included, is one character per cell - the ring is
# drawn two cells thick, the same number the spawner reads from kFortressRingCells.
#
# A level sends 20 enemies, each one a random model, unless a line like "20 enemies: 4 basic, fast"
# says otherwise: the number is how many enemies the level has, the list after the colon gives the
# models of the first ones in the order they come - "4 armor" for four in a row, a bare "fast" for
# one - and every enemy past the list is rolled. The list may be left out: "7 enemies:" sends seven
# random ones. The models are basic, fast, power and armor.
#
# Lines starting with # and blank lines are ignored, so feel free to sketch here.
